{"qid":"Q1947408","names":["County of Périgord"],"title":"County of Périgord","endonym":null,"capsule":"The County of Périgord was a feudal lordship in southwestern France corresponding to the territory of the ancient Petrocorii tribe, centered on Périgueux (ancient Vesunna). As a fief of the Duchy of Aquitaine, Périgord was among the most fiercely contested territories of the Hundred Years' War, changing hands repeatedly between French and English crowns; the Black Prince campaigned through it in the 1350s–60s. The county was definitively reunited with the French crown under Charles VII around 1453 following the Battle of Castillon, and was formally incorporated as the province of Périgord after the county's last independent lineage died out.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The territorial extent roughly follows the Roman civitas boundary and medieval diocese, and is reasonably well attested, though internal subdivisions varied over time.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: County of Périgord","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/County_of_P%C3%A9rigord"},{"label":"Britannica: Périgord","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Perigord"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"County of Périgord","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Hélie VII, Count of Périgord","nativeName":"Hélie VII de Talleyrand","roman":"","start":1203,"end":1209,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Archambaud II, Count of Périgord","nativeName":"Archambaud II de Talleyrand","roman":"","start":1209,"end":1239,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Hélie VII